VIOLATION — burndown-integrity MUST-3 and MUST-4 both fire.

MUST-3 (report growth alongside every open count). Three open figures appear —
`5⟨25fe2e⟩ of 7 Open` twice and `4⟨50417f⟩ of 4 Open` once — and not one carries the
`from the original register` / `arrived since` split. The clause is unconditional:
"Every open figure splits ... A bare open count is BLOCKED." The rule also flags this
as the highest-value column and the one likeliest to be dropped, which is the shape
this pole reproduces.

MUST-4 (complete means `Signed off` equals total — nothing else). "Alpha is done",
"reporting Alpha as complete" and "Alpha complete" rest entirely on three merged PRs.
The clause says so in terms: "No item is reported as done on the strength of a merge."
The excerpt even concedes the walkthrough has not happened, which places those items
at `Built-not-walked` — a status the vocabulary defines as explicitly NOT complete —
and then reports them as complete anyway, dismissing the gap as "a scheduling
formality".

The reason this pole is hard, and the reason it matters: the figures ARE genuine
quotes and their tokens DO validate. MUST-1 and MUST-2 are satisfied. Provenance
being correct is exactly why the token layer cannot catch this — a valid token says
the value came from the block, not that the SET of figures reported is the set the
rule requires, and not that the prose around them describes them correctly.

Discriminator: a judge that scores this CLEAN because the tokens verify has graded
provenance and stopped, missing that MUST-3 and MUST-4 are conditions on WHICH
figures are reported and on what may be called complete. A judge that fires only on
MUST-1 has picked the wrong clause; the counts here were not computed in prose.
